# Findings of `make aws-scan` that are accepted for this module, which is a test
# environment applied once and removed within hours (S036). The acceptances are
# the session's decisions of 2026-10-06: the owner sees them before any apply
# and may overturn any of them.
#
# One Trivy check ID per line, exactly `AWS-` and four digits and nothing after
# it (no expiry, no file name, no wildcard, not the older AVD- spelling), and on
# the line directly above it the reason: what the check says, why it holds here,
# and what a production environment would do instead, in a handful of words at
# least. tests/meridian/test_aws_scan.py fails on an entry that breaks any of
# this, on an inline ignore comment in a .tf file, on a Trivy configuration or
# YAML ignore file in this directory, and on a second cluster or subnet block:
# an entry here applies to EVERY resource of the directory, not to one. A
# finding fixed in the module is deleted from here.

# AWS-0040 (critical), "Public cluster access is enabled": the public endpoint is open to one address, the applying machine's /32, and the private endpoint is on as well. Terraform itself needs only the EKS API (the AWS API, reached with the owner's credentials), not this endpoint; the public side is there for kubectl and anything else that talks to the cluster itself, which with it off would have to come from inside the VPC or a connected network, and this environment has no bastion and no VPN. Production: private access only, reached through a bastion or a VPN.
AWS-0040

# AWS-0039 (high), "Cluster does not have secret encryption enabled": the check asks for an encryption_config with a customer-managed KMS key. EKS already encrypts all Kubernetes API data on a cluster at Kubernetes 1.28 or later (this one is at 1.36) with envelope encryption by default, and the key is AWS-owned. A customer-managed key waits at least seven days to be removed and bills meanwhile, the environment lives for an hour, and nothing in this module stores a Kubernetes Secret. Production: an encryption_config with a customer-managed KMS key.
AWS-0039

# AWS-0164 (high), "Subnet associates public IP address": the subnets give their instances public addresses. The design has no NAT gateway, because one bills by the hour and survives a removal that stops half way; the nodes' security group admits nothing from the internet. Production: private subnets behind a NAT gateway or VPC endpoints.
AWS-0164
